  "findings": [
    {
      "claim": "A person's answer to a judgement question is not saved in conflict.json, the per-goal file the design names. Only the question id is saved, and the answer is read back from the question record of whichever checkout runs the command. If `work rebase` (or `work land`, which runs it first) runs from a different checkout of the same goal worktree than the one that asked (for example the goal worktree itself instead of the main checkout), the question record is not there. The rebase then aborts with a raw 'no such file or directory' error that names no next step. It aborts again on every run from that checkout while the conflict keeps the same blob ids. This breaks the design's 'Shared state is keyed by goal (conflict.json with its answer ...) never by checkout' and 'The answer is recorded in conflict.json with its impact'. The person's answer cannot reach the resolve round from that checkout. The fix is to save the answer text into conflict.json, or to treat a missing question as unanswered and ask again, instead of aborting.",
      "evidence": "Traced path in the reviewed tree. (1) The first stop on a judgement conflict calls rebaseJudgementQuestions. That function writes conflict.json under inv.goalBranchInstallation(id) (cmd/metasystem/intent_work_rebase.go:244). goalBranchInstallation returns the same goal worktree for any checkout of the clone (intent_delivery.go:1222-1236). The entry it writes has \"resolution\": \"\" and \"question\": asked.ID (intent_work_rebase.go:271-273). The question itself is asked into inv.layout.InstallationRoot (line 270), the invoking checkout, and stored under that checkout's artifacts/agents/channel/questions/<id>.json (internal/channel/question.go:88-91). (2) The next run from another checkout reads the same conflict.json. When the blob ids still match, it calls req.Answer(answer.Question) (internal/goal/branch/rebase_conflict.go, in the answers loop of resolveRebaseStop). Answer calls channel.ReadQuestion(inv.layout.InstallationRoot.Path(), question) (intent_work_rebase.go:110-118), which returns os.ReadFile's ENOENT error. (3) resolveRebaseStop returns abort(err). That error is not a RebaseConflict, so rebaseGoal's refused() puts the raw error text in the summary and never rewrites conflict.json (intent_work_rebase.go:63-79). Each later run from that checkout repeats steps 2 and 3. Nothing removes question files (no os.Remove in internal/channel), so this does not heal by itself. Running the same command from the serving engine inside the goal worktree is supported: `work status` from the goal worktree reported its installation as .../agentic-tools-m1h-conflicts-resolve-unattended/metasystem. No test covers the command-level Answer adapter or the conflict.json writer: the branch test pre-writes conflict.json with resolution \"keep main's\" and never goes through a channel question.",
      "id": "F-1-ANSWER-KEYED-BY-CHECKOUT",
      "material": true,
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The commit changes an existing public-verb test that has nothing to do with resolve rounds, TestWorkRebaseGitAdapterHoldsAfterHistory. Before every `work rebase` or `work land` call, the test now fetches and accepts main's ledger itself (goal.FetchAdvance). The command under test therefore no longer does the fetch and validation of main's move, or of its own just-published history line; the test has already done them. The test exists to prove that work after the history publication moved main holds. This change narrows what it certifies, and the test passes at this commit without it.",
      "evidence": "Diff hunk in metasystem/cmd/metasystem/intent_delivery_owner_test.go:435-444 adds branch.MainEndpoint plus goal.FetchAdvance inside run(), with a comment saying the command's Stop deadline 'does not measure this fixture's validation'. I extracted the commit's tree to $TMPDIR and restored the parent's version of that test file. `go test ./cmd/metasystem/ -run TestWorkRebaseGitAdapterHoldsAfterHistory -count=1` printed ok (27.7s). `-run 'TestWorkRebaseGitAdapterHoldsAfterHistory|TestWorkLand' -count=3` printed ok (103.3s). This unit changes nothing on that test's path: no conflict occurs there, so neither Resolve nor Answer is called.",
